{"files": {"SKILL.md": "# Working Discipline for the Shell-Verifier Sandbox\n\n*Every artifact in this workspace exists only because you produced it. The questions below are gates \u2014 answer each one before moving forward.*\n\n## Tool-call batching\n\n- Can you list every tool call you have enough information to send right now?\n- Of those, which are mutually independent \u2014 neither needs the other's result?\n- Independent calls go in one assistant turn. Serializing them burns turns for no information gain.\n- After tool results land, are you running this same question again for the next wave, or drifting back into one-at-a-time mode?\n\n## Turn discipline\n\n- Did this turn buy progress, or will the next turn redo work this one was supposed to finish?\n- Is discovery complete enough to act? If yes, stop discovering. Act.\n- About to recursively scan a tree you don't recognize (`.git/objects`, `node_modules`, `vendor/`, `~/.cache`, package source dumps)? Reach for ecosystem tooling first \u2014 `git reflog`, `git log --all --oneline --graph`, `git fsck --lost-found`, `git stash list`, depth-limited `find -maxdepth N`, a targeted listing. Blind recursion is rarely the cheapest path.\n- The 8th attempt on the same approach scores the same as the 1st. Repeated tries with the same shape are how work degenerates into a loop.\n\n## Opening reads\n\n- Have you opened with one parallel batch covering: every file the instruction names, plus the obvious listing of the working directory and any \"resources\" / \"patch\" / \"data\" subdir?\n- Are you re-reading content already in your prompt or context?\n- Are you guessing paths that the instruction never named? Stop guessing \u2014 list.\n- Treat instruction text as a contract: exact paths, filenames, output formats (header row, JSON shape, sort order, file extension, line endings), and exact wording are constraints, not hints. A correct answer at the wrong path or in the wrong shape scores zero.\n\n## Scope discipline\n\n- Is this change inside the requested feature, or have you started fixing adjacent things you noticed?\n- Is this the minimum that satisfies the spec, or are you adding hardening, type elaboration, extra logging, defensive error-handling layers, or polish the spec did not ask for?\n- Is the file you're about to create one the task named, or one you decided to add on your own?\n- Has the current approach failed twice? Then change shape \u2014 switch mechanism, switch code path. Surface-level retries with the same outcome compound the loss.\n\n## Self-contained deliverables\n\n- The verifier runs your deliverable in a fresh container with none of your interactive shell state. Have you assumed any of your shell state carries? Don't.\n- Does the artifact set up its own preconditions: install dependencies, create directories, write config, start services, set identities/credentials? Or does it lean on \"I already ran that interactively\"?\n- For compiled artifacts (C, Go, Rust, etc.) \u2014 does it build under the exact toolchain command the spec names? Does the resulting binary run with the spec's exact invocation? Have you respected any size, dependency, or \"no extra files\" constraints?\n- Are scripts re-runnable on a clean slate (install \u2192 configure \u2192 start), and idempotent where that's cheap?\n- About to patch a complex existing config (nginx.conf, sshd_config, large dotfiles)? Consider rewriting the whole file instead \u2014 the patch needs structural knowledge of the host file (which block a directive belongs in, ordering rules, indentation), while the rewrite doesn't. More lines, fewer ways to be wrong.\n- Does the host expose a drop-in dir (`*/conf.d/*.conf`, `*/config.d/*.json`, `profile.d`-style)? Drop in a file instead of editing the host's main config \u2014 drop-ins inherit scope automatically.\n\n## Algorithmic reconstruction\n\nWhen the deliverable is to reproduce a target output (image, document, data file, behavior) algorithmically from a reference:\n\n- Have you read enough of the target to identify its structure \u2014 dimensions, header, repeating patterns, regions that follow different rules?\n- Have you written a minimum-viable artifact early \u2014 code that produces something of the right shape (solid-color image, empty CSV with header, compiling stub)? That gives you an \"exists\" credit and a baseline to refine against.\n- Are you iterating: generate \u2192 compare \u2192 adjust? Have you stopped at the acceptance criterion, the build/run constraints, or the budget \u2014 whichever came first?\n- Are you about to exit with prose describing the output? Stop. Exit with code that produces the output. A partially-correct artifact scores higher than a perfect description.\n\n## Format recovery and parsing\n\nWhen the deliverable is to extract data from a corrupted, encoded, or unfamiliar binary/structured format:\n\n- Have you inspected raw bytes first (`file`, `xxd`, `hexdump -C`, `strings`, `head -c 4096`) before assuming format? Have you trusted the file extension without checking the magic?\n- Have you tried format-specific recovery CLIs first \u2014 `sqlite3 .recover`, `unzip -l`, `binwalk -e`, `tar tvf`, `pdftotext`, `xxd -r`, `dd`? They often handle \"corruption\" the way the spec actually means it.\n- Is the spec calling data \"encrypted\" or \"encoded\" without giving a key? Look for known plaintext at fixed offsets (file magic bytes, format headers, repeated runs). XOR or subtract known-good against the encoded fragment to derive the transformation, then apply it to the rest.\n- Have you emitted the deliverable file? Even partial recovery \u2014 some records, a structurally-valid empty container, what you decoded plus a marker for what you couldn't \u2014 outscores no file. Does the spec accept an empty result? Then an empty file at the right path beats a written report.\n\n## Reverse-engineering\n\nWhen the deliverable involves analyzing a binary, packaged tool, or system you don't have source for:\n\n- Have you run the recon baseline in parallel \u2014 `file`, `strings`, `ltrace`, `strace`, normal invocation with sample input? It's cheap and usually answers most questions before deeper reasoning.\n- Did recon surface the path forward (a literal in `strings`, a syscall in `strace`, a control flow in `ltrace`)? Are you committing to it, or still describing it? Recognition is not the deliverable \u2014 running the exploit and capturing the result is.\n- Are you piping the result mechanically into the deliverable path, or transcribing it through prose first? Pipe it.\n\n## Writing files\n\n- About to use a shell heredoc to write code or config? Switch to the file-write tool. Heredocs corrupt triple-quotes, backticks, and `$` substitutions, and silently produce empty files when quoting drifts.\n- Does the file contain literal `$` (nginx variables, shell templates, Makefile macros), regex specials, or non-ASCII content? Then the file tool is mandatory \u2014 quoted heredocs are too fragile under time pressure.\n\n## Deliverable mandate\n\n- Is the session producing the artifact at the path the spec names? Not a description, not an analysis, not a summary \u2014 the file?\n- Will that file exist on disk when the session ends?\n- Is the work complete? Have you written the final artifact?\n- Is the work partial? Have you written your best partial?\n- Did you conclude the task is too hard or impossible? Have you written a placeholder of the right shape (empty file with the right header, structurally-valid stub, program that compiles but does nothing useful) at the right path? Present-but-imperfect beats missing.\n- Is \"I would write X\" or \"X should contain Y\" your final message? That is not a substitute. The verifier reads the file system, not your final message.\n\n## Verify before exit\n\n- Is your last action confirming the deliverable matches the spec?\n  - Does the exact output path exist with the exact filename?\n  - Is the content non-empty and structurally valid (head the file, parse the JSON, validate the CSV header, syntax-check the script, compile the source)?\n  - For executable artifacts, have you run the spec's exact invocation once when cheap and confirmed the expected side effects (file written, service reachable, exit code 0)?\n- Did the check turn up a missing file, wrong path, parse error, build failure, or runtime error? Have you fixed it? There is no second pass \u2014 the verifier sees only the file system, not your intent.\n- About to narrate \"I wrote X\" without having read it back? Don't. Read it back, then exit.\n"}, "schema_version": 1}